WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.397
A café owner's phone buzzes: payment received, 30.00.

2
00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:11.239
She opens the payments app to look at it, and the payment isn't in the list.

3
00:00:11.239 --> 00:00:13.827
She pulls down to refresh, and there it is.

4
00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:16.981
The message was sent after the payment was saved.

5
00:00:16.981 --> 00:00:19.776
The list read a copy that hadn't caught up yet.

6
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:21.222
It's easy to shrug.

7
00:00:21.222 --> 00:00:23.349
A second later, everything agreed.

8
00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:31.232
But if the app had used that list to decide whether the payment could be refunded, a second is plenty of time to decide wrong.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.101
The usual cure is to make the copies behave like one.

10
00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:37.615
So what exactly does one copy demand?

11
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.546
Picture the departure board at a station.

12
00:00:40.546 --> 00:00:41.191
One board.

13
00:00:41.191 --> 00:00:45.451
At some instant, your train's line flips from on time to boarding.

14
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.690
Anyone who looks before the flip sees on time.

15
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.169
Anyone after, boarding.

16
00:00:50.169 --> 00:00:55.183
If you glance up right while it changes, you get one or the other, never half.

17
00:00:55.433 --> 00:01:00.243
And once anybody has seen boarding, nobody who looks later can see on time.

18
00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:01.911
A board doesn't flip back.

19
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:02.723
Map it.

20
00:01:02.723 --> 00:01:04.367
The board is one copy.

21
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:07.729
The flip is the instant a write takes effect.

22
00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:09.672
And each glance is a read.

23
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.046
Now four programs and one value.

24
00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:15.870
The payments app keeps each payment's status as a number.

25
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.553
1 means authorized, the money set aside.

26
00:01:18.553 --> 00:01:21.639
2 means captured, the money moved to the shop.

27
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.052
The capture program writes status 2, and the write takes 60 milliseconds from request to answer.

28
00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:32.025
A value you can read and write like this is called a register.

29
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.923
Meanwhile, three programs read the status.

30
00:01:34.923 --> 00:01:37.046
The ledger, which keeps the books.

31
00:01:37.046 --> 00:01:38.170
The fraud checker.

32
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.918
And the email program, which sends receipts.

33
00:01:40.918 --> 00:01:45.539
Each bar runs from when the request is sent to when the answer comes back.

34
00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:48.498
Here's the board's rule, written for bars.

35
00:01:48.498 --> 00:01:52.984
Pick one instant inside each bar where the operation really happened.

36
00:01:52.984 --> 00:01:56.625
Then replay those instants, in order, on one plain copy.

37
00:01:56.625 --> 00:01:58.316
Every answer has to match.

38
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.807
The ledger, from 5 to 15, got the old status.

39
00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:03.162
The dash means old.

40
00:02:03.162 --> 00:02:07.367
Fine: put its instant at 10, and the write's instant later.

41
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:10.405
The fraud checker, from 20 to 30, got 2.

42
00:02:10.405 --> 00:02:14.423
So the write's instant must come before the fraud check's.

43
00:02:14.423 --> 00:02:17.680
Put the write at 18, and the fraud check at 25.

44
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:20.722
The capture program hasn't heard back yet.

45
00:02:20.722 --> 00:02:22.033
That doesn't matter.

46
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:26.601
Now the email program, from 40 to 50, got the old status.

47
00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:28.473
Can we place its instant?

48
00:02:28.473 --> 00:02:29.671
Pause and guess.

49
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:33.118
No.

50
00:02:33.118 --> 00:02:37.049
Its instant must be after 40, so it's after the write.

51
00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:39.233
One copy would answer 2 there.

52
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:41.271
No choice of instants works.

53
00:02:41.271 --> 00:02:43.600
This history isn't linearizable.

54
00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:49.387
Once any read has returned the new value, every read that starts later must return it too.

55
00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:55.130
You might be thinking: the fraud checker and the email program run on different computers.

56
00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.680
If they never talk, who would ever notice?

57
00:02:57.967 --> 00:02:59.790
Any second channel notices.

58
00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.356
The fraud checker announces: captured.

59
00:03:02.356 --> 00:03:05.799
The email program reacts, and reads the old status.

60
00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:09.108
That's the café owner's buzz, and her empty list.

61
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:11.646
It's a friend texting: we're boarding!

62
00:03:11.646 --> 00:03:14.750
while the board in front of you still says on time.

63
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.941
One of you is about to miss a train.

64
00:03:16.941 --> 00:03:22.480
So the order that matters is real time, because any message between programs can expose it.

65
00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:32.607
Had the email program read 2, the instants would be: the ledger at 10, the write at 18, the fraud check at 25, and the email at 45.

66
00:03:32.607 --> 00:03:34.416
Each inside its own bar.

67
00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.077
Replay them on one copy, and every answer matches.

68
00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:44.520
That order is called a linearization, and each chosen instant is a linearization point.

69
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.279
A history that has one is linearizable.

70
00:03:47.279 --> 00:03:51.805
Maurice Herlihy and Jeannette Wing gave this exact rule in 1990.

71
00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:53.888
Two things to keep straight.

72
00:03:53.888 --> 00:03:56.229
The instant is a thought experiment.

73
00:03:56.229 --> 00:03:57.985
The store never reports it.

74
00:03:57.985 --> 00:04:00.456
We only ask whether some choice works.

75
00:04:00.733 --> 00:04:02.999
And the word seems carries the weight.

76
00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:06.577
Inside, a store may hold writes back, copy them, retry them.

77
00:04:06.577 --> 00:04:11.527
What it may not do is let any client see something one copy couldn't have produced.

78
00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:15.277
One gift from the proof: the rule works value by value.

79
00:04:15.277 --> 00:04:22.169
If the operations on each value pass, the whole history passes, so a checker can take each value on its own.

80
00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:27.403
And sending each write to every copy, sooner or later, doesn't give you this rule.

81
00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.569
So: one copy, one timeline.

82
00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:38.731
Every operation lands at one instant inside its bar, and once anyone sees the new value, nobody who starts later sees the old one.

83
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.229
One quick check.

84
00:04:40.229 --> 00:04:48.297
Suppose the email's read had run from 22 to 32, overlapping the fraud check, and returned the old status.

85
00:04:48.297 --> 00:04:48.912
Allowed?

86
00:04:48.912 --> 00:04:50.141
Pause and think.

87
00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:53.687
Yes.

88
00:04:53.687 --> 00:04:58.806
Put the email at 23, the write at 24, and the fraud check at 26.

89
00:04:58.806 --> 00:05:02.965
A read that overlaps the change may see either side.
